# Portfolio v6 — History and lessons

> Current architecture is in `architecture.mdc`. This is the log: what was
> tried, what broke and why, so the same ground isn't re-broken. Entries about
> code that no longer exists stay only while the mistake is still reachable.

## What v6 is
A gallery of composed artworks. It grew out of v5 (the collage room), and takes
v5's project screenshots (`src/assets/projects/`, `src/data/projectImages.ts`)
but none of its room. Every experiment is built **from a reference image Jin
supplies**: a mock, a photo or a screenshot of an effect. The job is to reproduce
that image's look, then expose the feel-tunables in a tilde panel.

## Experiments, and what each one taught

Which ones are studio layers: grid, blueprint, terminal, paths, scatter,
cursors, collage and map (`studio/registry.ts`). **Poster, glass, droplet and
cloud are page-only.** They own a three.js scene and need porting to the
compositor's shared renderer first (deferred; see `architecture.mdc`).

- **Collage.** Torn paper pasted down piece by piece, with click-to-rip. The
 palette went vivid on a reference (orange, cobalt, violet, pink, coral, sky,
 yellow, green). The store was renamed `collage-v2` so old saved tuning
 couldn't drag the new palette back. ~96% of pieces are generated prints
 (`prints.ts`): colour, gradient, halftone dot screen or speckle, scribble,
 geometry, type, ruler and drawing. Only `imageShare` (0.04) uses real images:
 first screenshots of the hand-kept brand/art list in
 `terminal/imagePool.ts` (product UI excluded on purpose). Scraps left by a rip
 go back in at the ripped sheet's stack index, not on top.
- **City map.** Line-drawn streets with dotted walking routes. Routes first
 **stacked on one avenue**. Fixed by rejecting a candidate path that runs within
 5× dot size of existing dots (`crowded`) and dropping the road share to 0.3.
- **Water drops (droplet).** Draw a shape and it becomes refracting water. The
 mask is drawn at 0.5 scale, blurred twice into a HalfFloat target, and the
 water shader reads its gradient as the surface normal. The first caustic read
 as a **white halo**, fixed with caustics .6 → .2 and shadow .5 → .6. ⌘Z undoes
 the last stroke.
- **Cloud timelapse.** A woodblock cumulus that boils in place. v1 read as
 **stacked balls with a thick white band, too dark, with floating lobes**:
 - Too dark: the colour-space bug below.
 - Balls: replaced by a soft-max height blend across lobes, plus fbm normal
 perturbation.
 - White band: fixed in the lace threshold.
 - Floating lobes: lobes now rise from a skeleton column of four base lobes.
- Earlier ones (poster, grid, terminal, glass, blueprint, paths, scatter,
 cursors) predate this log. Their pages and controls are the record.

## Bugs already paid for

- **WebGL colours came out dark.** The renderer's `outputColorSpace` is
 Linear, and a `THREE.Color` set from a hex string gets converted
 sRGB → linear. Colour uniforms must use
 `color.setStyle(hex, THREE.LinearSRGBColorSpace)` so the hex passes through
 as written. It bit the cloud and the droplet; check any new three.js
 experiment for it. Canvas textures are left at their default colour space so
 their pixels also pass straight through. The compositor does the same,
 keeping sRGB values throughout so blend modes behave like a design tool's.
- **Renderers must take values as arguments**, never read a store's `.values`.
 Otherwise retuning an experiment page silently changes saved artworks.
 `glassRenderer` still violates this (noted in `architecture.mdc`).
- **The shape shader can't early-return.** `fwidth` needs uniform control flow,
 and `LAYER_FRAG` uses it for antialiasing every distance-field edge.
- **Shape styles get clipped without room.** Node path's stickers and labels
 reach past the outline, so a shape style renders with an extra `STYLE_PAD` (90)
 beyond the edge pad. Its first render cut the stickers off at the frame.
- **Samplers need a real texture even when unused.** A plain-rectangle layer
 never reads its field or boolean samplers, but they're still bound to a 1×1
 `blank` texture.

## Workflow traps

- **Run the checks separately, never as `prettier && tsc && eslint`.** When
 Prettier fails to parse a file it exits non-zero and the chain silently skips
 type-check and lint. A duplicated line shipped to the dev server that way
 while the checks looked clean.
- **Prettier rewrites code an edit is anchored to.** After `npx prettier
 --write src`, re-read before the next StrReplace.
- **Vite's error overlay replays stale errors into screenshots.** A fresh
 headless page can show an error that's already fixed. Remove
 `vite-error-overlay` in `PRE`/`EVAL` before judging a shot.
- **Screenshot tooling** (`scripts/shoot.mjs`). It needs to run outside the
 sandbox (Chrome). Env vars:
 - `STORE` seeds one localStorage key, e.g. a studio draft at `studio:draft:`.
 - `PRE` runs before the drag, e.g. to pick a tool:
 `dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent("keydown",{key:"s"}))`.
 - `EVAL` runs after the drag, before the shot.
 - The tilde panel can be hidden with a `` ` `` keydown.

 Window-dispatched key events have no element target, so key handlers guard
 with `typing()` from `studio/tools.ts`.
- **Fast refresh:** a component file may only export components. Shared tool
 types and helpers live in `studio/tools.ts` for that reason.
- **The react-hooks lint rule flags ref access in render-time callbacks.** That
 includes handlers created inside a `.map` that reach a ref. Get the element
 from the event instead (`e.currentTarget.ownerSVGElement`).

## The studio became a shape tool (Nov 2026)
Jin wanted to stop going back and forth between experiments and works. Layers
became **shapes filled with a shader**, with experiments renamed "shaders" in
the UI. The plan, in agreed order:
1. Shapes, freeform drawing, vertex editing, smear, solid/gradient/shader fills
 and edge effects (paper rip, stroke, feather, shadow). Done.
2. Node path as a shape style. Done.
3. Booleans (unify, intersect, subtract, exclude). Done.
4. Liquify: a smear brush for any layer's pixels, not just outlines.
5. The WebGL shaders (cloud, glass, poster, and droplet as a surface effect
 refracting the layers below) on the compositor's shared renderer.

The key design decision was **one signed distance field per shape**, rather
than canvas clipping plus separate effect passes. Tear, paper core, stroke,
feather, shadow and booleans all read the same distance, so they compose for
free. A stroke follows the torn edge; a shadow is the torn, combined shape
offset. A layer with no shape is an exact analytic rectangle, so artworks
saved before shapes existed render as before (verified on `field-notes`).

## Open questions from design talks
- **Raised text on the site** (neumorphism, from a Figma mock): feasible with
 `text-shadow` alone. The open issue is readability. Ground-coloured text has
 near-zero contrast, so body copy wants a slightly darker fill than the
 headline.
- **Water drops across the real page.** WebGL can't read DOM pixels.
 - `backdrop-filter: url(#svg-filter)` with a displacement map bends the live
 page, but only in Chromium.
 - Safari/Firefox would need `filter: url()` on the page wrapper with one
 page-sized map: costly on iOS, and the drops scroll with the content.

 Not built; a side-by-side test page in Safari was the proposed next step.

## Jin's preferences
- Brings references as images and expects the look matched, then tunes it
 himself in a tilde panel. Build the panel rather than guessing at constants.
- Verify visual work by screenshot, and look at the shot before reporting.
- Wants to understand *why* before something is built, and appreciates
 TypeScript explanations.
- Aesthetic: printed matter, paper and woodblock textures, technical drawing,
 monospace small caps. Vivid colour only where a reference asks for it.
